[size=5][b]New Creature - Werewolf[/b][/size]

Adds a werewolf - a new late-game predator that hunts you at night. Fast enough that running is not an answer, brutal against walls, and it tears zombies apart too. Comes in three coats, one per biome, and can howl for backup.

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]

Nobody agrees on where they came from.

Some say the wolves were the first to drink from the rivers after the bombs, and that whatever was in the water finished what the radiation started. Others swear they were people once - the ones who kept running north when everyone else dug in, until the winter and the hunger made them into something that never needed a base at all.

The traders do not care either way. They quote the going rate for the pelt, glance at the door, and remind you they close at dusk.

What everyone does agree on is this: the horde is loud, slow and predictable. [b]This thing hunts.[/b]

[size=4][b]Beware the night![/b][/size]

The werewolf only hunts after dark (22:00-04:00) - but if you have run into one, you will remember it. It is furious, it is strong and it is [i]fast[/i]. It hates zombies every bit as much as it hates you, and it will happily tear them apart if they are stupid enough to touch it. Outrunning it is close to impossible - but if you put it down, the payout and the XP are worth every bullet.

[b]Shore up your base - it goes through walls about as well as it goes through faces.[/b]

On top of all that it has a howl, which calls another werewolf to its side (never more than two extra at once).

[size=4][b]What you are dealing with[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]It runs you down.[/b] Sprinting is not an escape plan. It is faster than you are, and faster still after dark. Break line of sight, get behind something solid, or fight.
[*][b]It hits like a zombie bear.[/b] Roughly that league for health and damage - except a bear does not chase you across a field at full tilt.
[*][b]It hears you.[/b] Shoot near one from behind a wall and it will come to the noise and commit to it. A wall you are shooting over is no longer a safe spot.
[*][b]It goes through walls.[/b] Not "eventually" - it is a brutal block breaker, and it will pick the wall over the long way round. Wood is a formality, concrete buys you time rather than safety.
[*][b]It hunts zombies too.[/b] Anything that scratches it gets torn up first - and it does not stop at one: it clears out everything crowding around it before it comes back for you. Bring it into a horde and watch what happens.
[*][b]The howl.[/b] A summon, not decoration: it rears up, roars, and another one comes in out of the dark to join the fight. The pack tops out at three, then the howl goes quiet for a while.
[*][b]Pack animals do not turn on each other.[/b] Their claws pass straight through their own kind, so do not count on them fighting it out.
[*][b]Worth the fight.[/b] A serious pile of XP, and a loot bag [b]every single time[/b] - the boss kind, not the scraps a regular zombie leaves. On top of that a full harvest: meat, leather, fat and bone, because the corpse butchers like an animal rather than like a rotting zombie.
[*][b]Three coats.[/b] Snow-white in the snow, sand-brown in the desert, near-black everywhere else.
[/list]

[size=4][b]When it shows up[/b][/size]

Not on day one, and that is deliberate. Spawns are gated behind [b]game stage 125[/b] - call it somewhere around [b]day 50[/b] for a survivor levelling at a normal pace - and only inside the night window. Before that the world spawns none at all, in any biome.

The idea is that it turns up right when you have stopped being afraid of the dark. It sits roughly a notch under the demolisher's gate and well above the zombie bear's, because unlike a horde night this one comes on an ordinary Tuesday while you are out looting.

Every biome is fair game once you are past the gate. There is a world-wide cap on how many wander around at once, so you get to [i]meet[/i] one rather than trip over a stream of them.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]7 Days to Die [b]V 3.2.0[/b] (built and tested against it; should work on any 3.x).
[*]The game must run [b]without EasyAntiCheat[/b] - the mod ships a DLL and EAC blocks those.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive.
[*]Extract it into your Mods folder so that you end up with [b]Mods\Werewolf\ModInfo.xml[/b].
[*]Start the game without EasyAntiCheat.
[/list]

Works on an existing save. To uninstall, delete the folder - any werewolf alive in your world goes with it.

[size=4][b]Multiplayer[/b][/size]

Not client side: the mod adds an entity and its assets, and both ends read those from their own files, so the [b]server and every client need the same version[/b]. Only tested in single player so far - feedback welcome.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

It adds a new creature and touches nothing that already exists - no vanilla entity, item or spawn group is changed - so it sits alongside other mods without argument.

Overhauls that replace the spawning system wholesale may not run its spawns; the creature itself still works, and the console still spawns one. [b]Undead Legacy has its own build[/b] - [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/7daystodie/mods/12581]Werewolf for Undead Legacy[/url] - which spawns properly there and is fitted to UL's own items and AI. Use that one on UL, not this one.

[size=4][b]Credits[/b][/size]

The model, its textures and its animations are a character asset licensed from Fab: [url=https://www.fab.com/listings/e6d3f2fc-f64d-418b-b41d-1f8808be3f21]fab.com/listings/e6d3f2fc-f64d-418b-b41d-1f8808be3f21[/url]. All credit for the artwork goes to its author. Everything else is the work of fitting it into 7 Days to Die - the rig, the animator, the prefab, the entity, its AI and its voice.

